{"term":"tradition","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:eb81e18e8d211d5dcb","definition":"A part of culture that is passed from person to person or generation to generation, possibly differing in detail from family to family, such as the way to celebrate holidays.","example":"After breakfast, Charles Macdoodle told Lady Mary that it was a tradition in the family that those rumbling carriages on the terrace betokened death.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["way","custom"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["antitradition","break with tradition","countertradition","mistradition","neotradition","oral tradition","protradition","subtradition","traditional","traditionalism","traditionally","traditionarily","traditionary","traditionate","traditionbound","traditionism","traditionist","traditionitis","traditionize","traditionless"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tradition#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:7867e9aed6fd936842","definition":"A commonly held system.","example":"They followed the tradition of lighting candles for special occasions.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["doctrine"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["antitradition","break with tradition","countertradition","mistradition","neotradition","oral tradition","protradition","subtradition","traditional","traditionalism","traditionally","traditionarily","traditionary","traditionate","traditionbound","traditionism","traditionist","traditionitis","traditionize","traditionless"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tradition#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:d9b3e2386c254afd13","definition":"An established or distinctive style or method","example":"Following tradition, the victorious athlete runs a lap around the track.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["antitradition","break with tradition","countertradition","mistradition","neotradition","oral tradition","protradition","subtradition","traditional","traditionalism","traditionally","traditionarily","traditionary","traditionate","traditionbound","traditionism","traditionist","traditionitis","traditionize","traditionless"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tradition#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:dea47da856e3215bd1","definition":"The act of delivering into the hands of another; delivery.","example":"A deed takes effect only from this tradition or delivery; for, if the date be false or impossible, the delivery ascertains the time of it.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["antitradition","break with tradition","countertradition","mistradition","neotradition","oral tradition","protradition","subtradition","traditional","traditionalism","traditionally","traditionarily","traditionary","traditionate","traditionbound","traditionism","traditionist","traditionitis","traditionize","traditionless"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tradition#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:6ebe831bbe5c4c6721","definition":"The entirety of a text's transmission; all the versions of a work.","example":"It is, for example, apparent, in light of further research and when considering more manuscripts, that the manuscript tradition of the Life of Kiros (a very widespread hagiographical text) is much more complex than he had thought (Marrassini 2004; see Krzyżanowska 2015).","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"u